String interning is a method of storing only one copy of each distinct string value, which must be immutable. The string interning pool is used to reduce the memory usage when you have many instances of the same string. 

In the .NET Framework, string interning isn't automatically applied when you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder. When you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder, a new string object is created in memory to hold the resulting string. This new string object is not in the intern pool, unless you explicitly add it with the String.Intern method. 

So in simple words, string concatenation or using StringBuilder doesn't change anything for the interned strings, they don't put the new string into intern pool either, unless explicitly asked to do so.

For instance:

string s = new StringBuilder().Append("Hello").Append(" ").Append("World").ToString();

// s is not added in the string intern pool

string poolString = string.Intern(s);

// Now s is added in the string intern pool

When you call String.Intern, it checks whether the string you are passing as an argument is already in the intern pool. If it's there, it returns a reference to that string. If it's not there, it adds the string to the pool and then returns a reference to it. This explicit call to intern is rare, but could be used in scenarios where you know you'll be using the same string repetitively and you want to save memory.

Remember that interning strings can be costly (in terms of time) so it should be done when the benefits clearly outweight the costs. In many cases, relying on the .NET runtime to manage memory for you (without interning strings) is sufficient and leads to cleaner, simpler code.

